        "url": "https://www.aap.org/en-us/advocacy-and-policy/aap-health-initiatives/practicing-safety/Pages/Temperament-Tips.aspx",
        "description": "Observing temperament Researchers describe infant temperament as varying along several distinct axes to give a profile that remains more or less stable over the child&#x27;s lifetime.1 These characteristics are: ... The qualities vary independently. For example, an active child may be more or less regular at bed or mealtimes. A sensitive child may adapt quickly to new situations or may take a long time. In any event, parents who try to change their child&#x27;s personality are in for a struggle.",
        "position": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "2 Month - 5 Early Relational Health Developmental Milestone Timeline",
        "url": "https://www.aap.org/en/patient-care/early-childhood/milestone-timeline/",
        "description": "Follow the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) Milestone Timeline to track and monitor your child&#x27;s early childhood development. Explore key milestones from birth to five years, including social, cognitive, language, and physical development. Gain valuable insights into your child&#x27;s growth ...",
        "position": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Infant Growth and Development | Pediatrics In Review | American Academy of Pediatrics",
        "url": "https://publications.aap.org/pediatricsinreview/article/18/7/224/36756/Infant-Growth-and-Development",
        "description": "This sequence is influenced continuously by intrinsic and extrinsic forces that produce individual variation and make each infant’s developmental path unique. Intrinsic influences include the child’s physical characteristics, state of wellness or illness, temperament, and other genetically determined attributes. Extrinsic influences during infancy originate primarily from the family: the personalities and style of caregiving by parents and siblings,the family’s economic status with its impact on resources of time and money, and the cultural milieu into which...",
        "position": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Promoting Optimal Development: Identifying Infants and Young Children With Developmental Disorders Through Developmental Surveillance and Screening | Pediatrics | American Academy of Pediatrics",
        "url": "https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/145/1/e20193449/36971/Promoting-Optimal-Development-Identifying-Infants",
        "description": "Close surveillance and earlier screening remains warranted if a child is at high risk for ASD, for example, if symptoms are present, the child has a sibling with ASD, the child has a genetic condition with known ASD risk, or the child has a history of prematurity or prenatal exposures (such as toxins or infection).47 Research shows that behaviors concerning for ASD emerge earlier than 18 months of age.37 Therefore, incorporation of surveillance for “red flags” into health supervision visits before formal screening at the 18- and 24-month visits is recommended.34 (Note: the USPSTF did not address high-risk individuals.)",
        "position": 5
      }
    ]
  }
}

